// polari-isle-test — THE THING THE PIPELINE ANALYSES (his ask 2026-09-19:
// "the testing isle being what the pipeline is analyzing. The pipeline
// should only generate artifact for things that are tested.").
//
// PREFLIGHT IS THE FIRST STAGE, always (his ask (A)): before the job
// touches the device it proves the device is CLEAR — no VM by our name, no
// Polari/isle footprint of its own — and has the RAM and disk the run
// needs. A FAIL there exits 4 and the job stops with the table in the log.
//
// THEN, ONE STAGE AT A TIME (CI_ISLE_STAGES in device.env, e.g.
// "core; household; electronics,cntfet"): for each stage, build that
// stage's app debs → stand a throwaway isle up → install the core debs and
// that stage's app debs → run the selftests INSIDE the installed product →
// record the result → TEAR THE ISLE DOWN, and only then start the next stage.
//
// ci-3 (his ask 2026-09-20) is the part of that sentence that used to be a
// marked TODO. Each half is now a NAMED stage of its own, so a run reads as a
// list of things that either happened or did not:
//
//   stage N · app debs        the stage's apps, from the ONE deb writer
//   stage N · payload         this run's core debs AND this run's own images.
//                             payload.sh REFUSES if an image is not on this
//                             daemon rather than letting the guest pull a
//                             published one — a verdict about an image we did
//                             not build is not a verdict.
//   stage N · throwaway up    the VM
//   stage N · install core    the prerequisites the deb does not depend on, the
//                             images loaded, `apt install polari-complete`, the
//                             .env pinned at the seed, and
//                             `ISLE_ASSUME_YES=1 isle core-install --skip-security`
//                             — then the WAIT for the isle to answer, which is
//                             what `install.ok` means (core-install ends on an
//                             echo, so its exit code proves nothing)
//   stage N · verify isle     from inside the guest: both routes 200,
//                             /api/health with its module count, the store, the
//                             router guest RUNNING under nested KVM, the CA
//   stage N · install apps    this stage's app debs, into the isle
//   stage N · selftests       the SAME suites `pol modules selftest` discovers,
//                             run with `docker exec prf-isle-backend`
//   stage N · uninstall …     unchanged (ci-10) — but now exercised for real
//   stage N · down + wipe / leak check   unchanged (ci-10)
//
// The arithmetic that turns those readings into `core_ok` lives in
// isle/results.py, NOT here: a Jenkinsfile cannot be executed by a selftest and
// this is the one number the release rule turns on.
// Sequential on purpose: a space-limited device never needs room for more
// than ONE isle at a time. That is the whole point of staging.
//
// A stage that fails does NOT abort the run: it is recorded as a failure,
// the isle is destroyed, and the next stage still runs — a later stage may
// pass, and what passed is exactly what may be released.
//
// ci-10 — THE TEARDOWN IS THREE THINGS, in this order, and they answer three
// different questions (his ask + addendum 2026-09-19):
//
//   1. `throwaway.sh uninstall`  — the PRODUCT'S OWN `isle uninstall
//      --everything`, run inside the guest as a de jure TEST of that path,
//      then its own zero-footprint verify and the hand-back proof (default
//      route, public DNS, apt, a network owner). A `dirty`/`failed` verdict is
//      a FAILURE OF THE PRODUCT and blocks the release: core_ok requires a
//      `clean` uninstall (routes/_lib.sh enforces it, not this file).
//   2. `throwaway.sh down`       — destroy + undefine + the per-run key, and
//      then `wipe`: everything tagged polari-ci-, and nothing else.
//   3. `leakcheck.sh check`      — a DIFF of the target against the baseline
//      taken before stage 1. Anything NEW that survived the wipe is a LEAK,
//      and so is RAM or disk that did not come back. That is a failure of THIS
//      PIPELINE, not of the product, and it is a resource guard, not a gate.
//
// A leak does NOT abort the run: it re-wipes, re-checks, and if the leak
// persists it is recorded and the run STOPS before the next stage — the next
// stage would otherwise start on a dirtier host than the last, which is
// exactly the "memory issues" the ask names. CI_LEAK_POLICY=continue opts out.
//
// The output is pool/<version>/isle-test/results.json, and THAT file is
// what Jenkinsfile.release and routes/_lib.sh read: nothing untested is
// ever published. ci-3 added the image IDs to it, so routes/_lib.sh can also
// refuse a release whose images are not the ones that were tested.
//
// WHERE the VM goes is device.env (CI_ISLE_TARGET=local|ssh). With
// CI_ISLE_TARGET=ssh the whole isle half happens on the other device over
// ssh and this controller only orchestrates.
//
// ⚠ LOCAL TARGET CAVEAT: this controller is a CONTAINER and libvirt lives on
// the host. The local path therefore needs either the libvirt socket mounted
// into the controller (a posture change nobody has authorised) or the job
// run by a host-tier agent. The ssh target has no such problem — which is
// the reason ci-7 makes the device configurable at all. The doctor says so.
pipeline {
  agent any
  // ci-12: this job locks the ISLE TARGET, not `polari-build`. It used to take
  // `polari-build` — but it is triggered with `wait: true` from a parent that is
  // ALREADY holding that lock (polari-release did; polari-test does now), so the
  // parent would wait for the child and the child for the parent. A real
  // deadlock, latent only because §75's release run died earlier. What this job
  // actually needs exclusive is the throwaway device, and that is what it takes.
  options { timestamps(); timeout(time: 180, unit: 'MINUTES'); disableConcurrentBuilds(); lock(resource: 'polari-isle-target') }
  parameters {
    string(name: 'VERSION', defaultValue: '', description: 'the pool version under test (empty = a bare device proof; no results are recorded against a release)')
  }
  environment {
    ISLE = "${env.POLARI_ISLE ?: '/var/polari-jenkins/isle'}"
    POOL = "${env.POLARI_POOL ?: '/var/polari-pool'}"
    JSONGET = '/var/polari-jenkins/jsonget.py'   // no readJSON: that plugin is not installed
  }
  stages {

    // ci-8: pull the settings the `cicd` app holds BEFORE the preflight reads them (non-fatal).
    stage('sync settings from Polari (non-fatal)') {
      steps { sh 'bash /var/polari-jenkins/cicd-sync.sh pull || true; bash /var/polari-jenkins/cicd-sync.sh run isle-test "$BUILD_NUMBER" running "$VERSION" "the throwaway isle stages" || true' }
    }

    stage('preflight — is the device clear, and does it have room?') {
      steps {
        sh 'bash "$ISLE/preflight.sh" --isle'          // exit 4 = REFUSED, with the table
        sh 'bash "$ISLE/preflight.sh" --isle --json > preflight.json; cat preflight.json'
      }
      post { always { archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'preflight.json', allowEmptyArchive: true } }
    }

    // ci-9: in APP MODE the core the stages install is PULLED, not built —
    // fetched once into the cache and verified against the release's own
    // SHA256SUMS. results.json then records `tested_against`, so "the app
    // passed" always names the core it passed against.
    //
    // ci-12 addendum 7 — THE CONDITION IS `CI_MODE == 'app'`, AND NOTHING ELSE.
    // It used to be `app mode OR CI_CORE_SOURCE startsWith release:`, and ci-9's
    // DEFAULT for CI_CORE_SOURCE is release:latest — which is in every
    // device.env, suite-mode ones included. So this stage fired on the pipeline
    // device, which is a suite device, and refused:
    //     [core-artifacts] REFUSED: polari-cli/scripts/lib/providers.sh is not
    //                      in this checkout
    // (isle-test #5). It was right to refuse — but it should never have been
    // asked: in suite mode the core under test is the one THIS RUN built, and
    // CI_CORE_SOURCE is an app-mode knob the suite path ignores.
    //
    // The checkout matters here too: this job has no `checkout` step at all (its
    // workspace is cleaned every run), so `providers.sh` — the ONE reader of
    // what a Polari release carries — is absent even in app mode. Init exactly
    // that submodule, and nothing else: a full forest checkout to read one
    // library would cost the six minutes the gate exists to avoid.
    stage('the core under test — pulled from a release (app mode)') {
      when { expression { return env.CI_MODE == 'app' } }
      steps {
        // the top level alone, then exactly ONE submodule. Anything more would
        // pay for the whole forest to read one shell library.
        checkout([$class: 'GitSCM', branches: [[name: '*/main']],
                  userRemoteConfigs: [[url: 'https://github.com/dausume/polari-suite.git']],
                  extensions: [[$class: 'CloneOption', shallow: true, depth: 1],
                               [$class: 'SubmoduleOption', disableSubmodules: true]]])
        sh '''set -eu
          git submodule update --init --depth 1 polari-cli
          ls -l polari-cli/scripts/lib/providers.sh'''
        script {
          def rep = params.VERSION ? "${env.POOL}/${params.VERSION}/cache-report.json" : "${env.WORKSPACE}/cache-report.json"
          // no Matcher in a local (CPS persists locals across step boundaries and
          // java.util.regex.Matcher is not serializable) — sed does the reading.
          env.CORE_TAG = sh(returnStdout: true,
                            script: "CACHE_REPORT='${rep}' bash \"\$ISLE/core-artifacts.sh\" fetch | sed -n 's/^CORE_TAG=//p'").trim()
          env.CORE_DEBS = sh(returnStdout: true,
                             script: "CACHE_REPORT='${rep}' bash \"\$ISLE/core-artifacts.sh\" fetch | sed -n 's/^CORE_DEBS=//p'").trim()
          echo "the stages below install the core of ${env.CORE_TAG ?: '(built here — CI_CORE_SOURCE=build)'}"
        }
      }
    }

    // ci-12 addendum 7 — SUITE MODE: the core is what this very run built, and
    // the debs are in the pool under the version the caller named:
    //   polari-test    VERSION=test/<sha>  →  pool/test/<sha>/debs
    //   polari-release VERSION=<version>   →  pool/<version>/debs
    // core-artifacts.sh owns that resolution for BOTH jobs (`built <version>`),
    // so neither Jenkinsfile composes a pool path of its own. An empty VERSION
    // is the bare-device proof: no pool directory, no core to install, and the
    // stage says so instead of failing.
    stage('the core under test — built by this run (suite mode)') {
      when { expression { return env.CI_MODE != 'app' && params.VERSION != '' } }
      steps {
        script {
          env.CORE_TAG  = 'this build'
          env.CORE_DEBS = sh(returnStdout: true,
                             script: "bash \"\$ISLE/core-artifacts.sh\" built '${params.VERSION}' | sed -n 's/^CORE_DEBS=//p'").trim()
          echo "the stages below install the core THIS RUN built: ${env.CORE_DEBS}"
        }
      }
    }

    stage('the testing stages') {
      steps {
        script {
          // device.sh owns the parsing; ask it, never re-split the string here.
          def raw = sh(returnStdout: true, script: 'bash -c \'source "$ISLE/../device.sh"; stages_list\'').trim()
          def stages = raw.split('\n').collect { it.trim() }
          echo "isle testing stages: ${stages.size()} — ${stages.collect { it ?: 'core only' }}"

          // ci-9: the core these stages install, named once, recorded everywhere.
          // ci-12 addendum 7: in SUITE mode that is always this build.
          def testedAgainst = env.CORE_TAG ?: ((env.CI_MODE == 'app')
                                               ? (env.CI_CORE_SOURCE ?: 'build')
                                               : 'this build')
          def coreDebs = env.CORE_DEBS ?: ''
          def runDir   = params.VERSION ? "${env.POOL}/${params.VERSION}/isle-test" : "${env.WORKSPACE}/isle-test"
          def RES      = "python3 '${env.ISLE}/results.py'"
          sh "mkdir -p '${runDir}'"

          // ci-10 — the reading everything below is diffed against. Taken ONCE,
          // before the first isle exists, so "new since the baseline" means
          // "this pipeline left it", not "the device carries it".
          def leakPolicy = (env.CI_LEAK_POLICY ?: 'stop')
          sh "CI_LEAK_DIR='${runDir}' bash \"\$ISLE/leakcheck.sh\" baseline"
          def stopped = ''

          stages.eachWithIndex { appsLine, i ->
            def idx     = i + 1
            def apps    = appsLine ? appsLine.split(' ').findAll { it } : []
            def appStr  = apps.join(' ')
            def started = new Date().format("yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss")
            def stageArgs = "--index ${idx} --apps '${appStr}' --started '${started}' " +
                            "--version '${params.VERSION ?: ''}' --tested-against '${testedAgainst}'"
            if (stopped) {
              // no reading files exist for this stage, and results.py says so in
              // words rather than defaulting it to something survivable.
              sh "${RES} stage '${runDir}' ${stageArgs} --error 'not run — ${stopped.replaceAll(/['\n]/, ' ')}'"
              return
            }

            def err = ''
            def leakVerdict = ''
            try {
              // ---- 1. the app debs this stage needs — built by the ONE builder
              //      (polari-framework appstore/custom/app_deb_builder.py).
              if (apps) {
                stage("stage ${idx} · app debs") {
                  sh "bash '${env.ISLE}/app-debs.sh' '${runDir}/stage-${idx}/debs' ${appStr}"
                }
              }

              // ---- 2. THE ARTIFACTS UNDER TEST, gathered: this run's core debs
              //      and this run's own images. payload.sh REFUSES if an image is
              //      not on this daemon rather than letting the guest pull a
              //      published one — a verdict about an image we did not build is
              //      not a verdict.
              stage("stage ${idx} · payload (the artifacts under test)") {
                sh "bash '${env.ISLE}/payload.sh' build '${runDir}/stage-${idx}/payload' '${coreDebs}' " +
                   (apps ? "'${runDir}/stage-${idx}/debs'" : "''")
              }

              // ---- 3. a fresh throwaway isle, for this stage alone
              stage("stage ${idx} · throwaway up") {
                sh 'bash "$ISLE/throwaway.sh" status; bash "$ISLE/throwaway.sh" up'
                sh 'bash "$ISLE/throwaway.sh" verify'
              }

              // ---- 4. ci-3: install the product, from the deb, as a person would.
              //      catchError, not `|| true`: a failed install must not abort the
              //      run (the reading IS the result) but must not look green either.
              stage("stage ${idx} · install core") {
                catchError(buildResult: 'SUCCESS', stageResult: 'UNSTABLE') {
                  sh "bash \"\$ISLE/throwaway.sh\" install --payload '${runDir}/stage-${idx}/payload' " +
                     "--json '${runDir}/install-${idx}.json' --stage ${idx}"
                }
              }

              // ---- 5. ci-3: is what is standing there an isle?
              stage("stage ${idx} · verify isle") {
                catchError(buildResult: 'SUCCESS', stageResult: 'UNSTABLE') {
                  sh "bash \"\$ISLE/throwaway.sh\" verify-isle --json '${runDir}/verify-${idx}.json' --stage ${idx}"
                }
              }

              // ---- 6. ci-3: this stage's apps, into the isle
              if (apps) {
                stage("stage ${idx} · install apps") {
                  catchError(buildResult: 'SUCCESS', stageResult: 'UNSTABLE') {
                    sh "bash \"\$ISLE/throwaway.sh\" apps --apps '${appStr}' " +
                       "--json '${runDir}/apps-${idx}.json' --stage ${idx}"
                  }
                }
              }

              // ---- 7. ci-3: THE TESTS, run inside the installed product.
              //      A core-only stage runs the framework's own suites; a stage
              //      that names apps runs those apps' suites.
              stage("stage ${idx} · selftests (inside the isle)") {
                catchError(buildResult: 'SUCCESS', stageResult: 'UNSTABLE') {
                  sh "bash \"\$ISLE/throwaway.sh\" selftests --modules '${apps ? appStr : 'core'}' " +
                     "--json '${runDir}/selftests-${idx}.json' --stage ${idx}"
                }
              }

            } catch (e) {
              err = e.toString()
              echo "stage ${idx} FAILED: ${e} — recording it and carrying on; a later stage may still pass"
            } finally {
              // ---- ci-10 layer 1: the PRODUCT's own uninstall, as a TEST. It runs
              // BEFORE the VM is destroyed and its verdict is independent of
              // everything below: a dirty hand-back is the product's failure, a
              // leak below is the pipeline's.
              stage("stage ${idx} · uninstall (the product's own)") {
                sh "bash \"\$ISLE/throwaway.sh\" uninstall --stage ${idx} --json '${runDir}/uninstall-${idx}.json' || true"
              }

              // ---- ci-10 layer 2: a throwaway that survives its stage is not a
              // throwaway. `down` now ends in a scoped `wipe`.
              stage("stage ${idx} · down + wipe") {
                sh 'bash "$ISLE/throwaway.sh" down || true'
              }

              // ---- ci-10 layer 3: prove it, by diff. A leak is re-wiped once and
              // re-checked before it is believed.
              stage("stage ${idx} · leak check") {
                def leakRc = sh(returnStatus: true,
                                script: "CI_LEAK_DIR='${runDir}' bash \"\$ISLE/leakcheck.sh\" check --stage ${idx}")
                if (leakRc == 5) {
                  echo "stage ${idx} LEAKED — re-wiping once and checking again before recording it"
                  sh 'bash "$ISLE/throwaway.sh" wipe || true'
                  leakRc = sh(returnStatus: true,
                              script: "CI_LEAK_DIR='${runDir}' bash \"\$ISLE/leakcheck.sh\" check --stage ${idx}")
                  leakVerdict = (leakRc == 5) ? 'leaked-after-rewipe' : 'clean'
                } else {
                  leakVerdict = (leakRc == 0) ? 'clean' : 'leak-check-unreadable'
                }
                if (leakVerdict == 'leaked-after-rewipe') {
                  if (leakPolicy == 'continue') {
                    echo "stage ${idx} leaked and a second wipe did not clear it — CI_LEAK_POLICY=continue, " +
                         "carrying on onto a host that is already dirtier than the baseline"
                  } else {
                    stopped = "stage ${idx} leaked and a second wipe did not clear it — " +
                              "STOPPING as a resource guard: the next stage would start on a dirty host. " +
                              "Set CI_LEAK_POLICY=continue to run anyway."
                    echo stopped
                  }
                }
              }

              // ---- the stage's record, assembled from the readings by the ONE
              // piece of arithmetic (isle/results.py) rather than in Groovy.
              def finished = new Date().format("yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss")
              sh "${RES} stage '${runDir}' ${stageArgs} --finished '${finished}' " +
                 "--leak-verdict '${leakVerdict}' --error '${err.replaceAll(/['\n]/, ' ')}'"
              def coreOk = sh(returnStdout: true,
                              script: "python3 ${JSONGET} '${runDir}/stage-${idx}.json' core_ok --default false").trim()
              // ci-8: mirror THIS stage into Polari as it finishes, so a long run is
              // legible while it is still running.
              sh """bash /var/polari-jenkins/cicd-sync.sh isle-test '${env.JOB_NAME}#${env.BUILD_NUMBER}' ${idx} ${coreOk} '${runDir}/stage-${idx}.json' || true"""
            }
          }

          // ---- the one file the release rule reads
          sh "${RES} assemble '${runDir}' --version '${params.VERSION ?: ''}' --mode '${env.CI_MODE ?: 'suite'}' " +
             "--tested-against '${testedAgainst}' --core-debs '${coreDebs}' --leak-policy '${leakPolicy}' " +
             "--stopped '${stopped.replaceAll(/['\n]/, ' ')}'"
          sh "cat '${runDir}/results.json'"
          // the pool is not the workspace: copy the readings in before archiving,
          // which is why every earlier run logged "No artifacts found".
          sh "mkdir -p isle-test && cp '${runDir}'/*.json isle-test/ 2>/dev/null || true"
          archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'isle-test/*.json', allowEmptyArchive: true
          if (stopped) {
            currentBuild.result = 'UNSTABLE'
            echo "the run stopped early: ${stopped}"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  post {
    always  {
      // belt and braces: nothing of OURS survives this job. `down` ends in the
      // scoped wipe; the extra `wipe` covers a job that died before any stage
      // ran (so `down` never happened) and is idempotent when it did.
      sh 'bash "$ISLE/throwaway.sh" down || true'
      sh 'bash "$ISLE/throwaway.sh" wipe || true'
      script { sh "bash /var/polari-jenkins/cicd-sync.sh run isle-test \"\$BUILD_NUMBER\" ${currentBuild.currentResult.toLowerCase()} \"\$VERSION\" 'stages recorded in results.json' || true" }
    }
    cleanup { cleanWs() }
  }
}
